{"id":5821,"date":"2019-05-08T15:50:02","date_gmt":"2019-05-08T19:50:02","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/?p=5821"},"modified":"2019-05-08T15:50:09","modified_gmt":"2019-05-08T19:50:09","slug":"so-just-what-is-fort-peck","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/2019\/05\/08\/so-just-what-is-fort-peck\/","title":{"rendered":"So Just What Is Fort Peck?"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p><strong>Fort Peck<\/strong> &#8211; It&#8217;s a <a href=\"https:\/\/www.visitmt.com\/places-to-go\/cities-and-towns\/fort-peck.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" aria-label=\"small town (opens in a new tab)\">small town<\/a> (about 250 residents) and it&#8217;s an Army Corp of Engineers &#8220;Project&#8221; that includes a dam, a campground, an Interpretive Center, and multiple fishing\/boating\/hunting recreation areas.  <\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Oh, and I almost forgot &#8230; the reason all of the above is here &#8230; the Fort Peck Reservoir.  The reservoir (man made lake) was formed from the Missouri River, is 135 miles long and has over 1500 miles of shoreline.  It was formed in the 30&#8217;s when 10,000+ men built the world&#8217;s largest earthen dam to provide flood control for lands downstream.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"760\" height=\"480\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/LakeMap.jpg?fit=685%2C433&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5822\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/LakeMap.jpg 760w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/LakeMap-300x189.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/LakeMap-768x485.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/LakeMap-1024x647.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/LakeMap-685x433.jpg 685w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 760px) 100vw, 760px\" \/><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>The dam and the history of how families came from all over the country to work there and the engineering that went into the planning and building &#8230; well that&#8217;s a story in itself and will require a blog post dedicated to that subject alone.  <a href=\"https:\/\/www.montanapbs.org\/programs\/fortpeckdam\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" aria-label=\"But you can learn all about that right now by following this link to PBS Montana. (opens in a new tab)\">But you can learn all about that right now by following this link to PBS Montana.<\/a><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>For now, we want to share with you a little about why we are here, how we came to find this job in particular, and take you on a tour of the park and show you some of our duties here.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Kathy and I are volunteers &#8230; well, kind of.  We travel the country volunteering our time at campgrounds and RV parks in <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">exchange<\/span> for the rent and utilities on our site.  We generally provide the park 12-15 hours a week and they give us a full hook-up (elec, water, sewer) site along with utilities.  Sometimes we also receive; laundry money, free WiFi and cable TV hook-up, and discounts on purchases from the camp store or nearby attractions.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>This arrangement offers us an opportunity to travel and see the country, meet all kinds of wonderful new people and experience new situations in all kinds of environments that we otherwise would not be able to see and do on our limited budget.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>It offers the campground owner\/operator free part-time employees for no cash outlay, only the loss of rental income for a couple RV sites that might often be vacant anyway.  Another benefit: typically there is no employment contract or agreement to sign &#8211; only a handshake agreement.  It&#8217;s called <a rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" aria-label=\"bartering (opens in a new tab)\" href=\"https:\/\/www.merriam-webster.com\/dictionary\/barter\" target=\"_blank\">bartering<\/a>.  And it works well for us and the campground owner.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-color has-background has-medium-font-size has-green-color has-white-background-color\"><strong>How we came to show up here .. <\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>We knew that part of our &#8220;Bucket List&#8221; included a lot of the national parks and monuments out west, so we decided we&#8217;d search for jobs in that area.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Although we subscribe, either for a small annual fee or sometimes for free, to many of the Workamping web sites and have our resume&#8217; published on a lot of them, there is also another great source for finding government related jobs and volunteer positions.  I logged on to <a rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" aria-label=\"Volunteer.gov (opens in a new tab)\" href=\"http:\/\/www.volunteer.gov\" target=\"_blank\">Volunteer.gov<\/a> and followed the easy instructions to search for Campground Host (volunteer) positions available in Idaho, Montana, Wyoming, Utah, and Colorado.  I filled out the online application and with just a few mouse clicks submitted our app to probably 40 or so parks in that five state area.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Within a week or two we started getting emails and phone calls from campground managers and rangers throughout the 5 state area.  We chose to come to Fort Peck because we were already committed to South Dakota for July, August, and September and Ranger Scott here at Fort Peck was willing to work with our schedule.  We left our leased RV lot in Casa Grande Arizona on April 1st and showed up at Fort Peck on April 15 just after the snow melted.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>It&#8217;s been fascinating to learn about the local history.  The town of Fort Peck was built by the corp to support the nearly 50,000 people that would come here to work on the building of the dam over the 10+ year period of 1930 to 1941.  There were as many as 11,000 men working on the dam at any one time during that period and eight men lost their lives during &#8220;the Big Slide&#8221; of 1938 and are permanently entombed in the dam to this day. <\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>To learn more about the dam building and how the town and local area and people from far and wide prospered during the depression, I encourage you to <a href=\"https:\/\/www.montanapbs.org\/programs\/fortpeckdam\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" aria-label=\"follow this link (opens in a new tab)\">follow this link<\/a> to visit the PBS web site with some great pictures from the era and a video about the building of the dam.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"760\" height=\"428\" src=\"https:\/\/i1.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104.jpg?fit=685%2C385&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5826\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104.jpg 760w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104-300x169.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104-768x432.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104-685x385.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190416_170104-2000x1125.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 760px) 100vw, 760px\" \/><figcaption>Our Camp Host Site on day one &#8211; April 15th<br><\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i2.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568.jpg?fit=685%2C514&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5827\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1568-2000x1500.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\" \/><figcaption>The theatre operated 24\/7 during construction of the dam with newsreels, movies and live plays to entertain the workers and their families and still offers summer plays to locals and visitors to the area.<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569.jpg?fit=685%2C514&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5828\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1569-2000x1500.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\" \/><figcaption>The Northeast Montana Veterans Memorial Park in the center of town<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630.jpg?fit=685%2C514&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5830\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1630-2000x1500.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\" \/><figcaption>The world&#8217;s largest earthen dam, 4 miles long, 3\/4 mile wide at the base, and 250&#8242; tall. <br><\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"760\" height=\"428\" src=\"https:\/\/i2.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916.jpg?fit=685%2C385&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5829\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916.jpg 760w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916-300x169.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916-768x432.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916-685x385.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/20190419_193916-2000x1125.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 760px) 100vw, 760px\" \/><figcaption>The Spillway located 3 miles east of the dam.  Yes, that&#8217;s ice on the downstream side<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i1.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561.jpg?fit=685%2C514&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5831\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1561-2000x1500.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\" \/><figcaption>The Interpretive Center contains displays information on the dam, local paleontology, and local fish and wildlife along with an educational movie theatre<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>There was a T-Rex discovered here (now on display at the Smithsonian) with a full-size replica here in the Interpretive Center.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i1.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742.jpg?fit=685%2C514&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5832\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/05\/IMG_1742-2000x1500.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\" \/><figcaption>The Fort Peck T-Rex in the Interpretive Center<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>Here&#8217;s a video that gives a tour of the campground and some of the surrounding area along with a little bit of what we&#8217;ve been doing at the park these first couple of weeks.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-embed-youtube wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio\"><div class=\"wp-block-embed__wrapper\">\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Camp Hosting at Fort Peck Montana Downstream Campground Tour\" width=\"685\" height=\"385\" src=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/embed\/P3_DrKbf2bA?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen><\/iframe>\n<\/div><figcaption>10 minute video on the Downstream Campground &#8211; Fort Peck Montana<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>Thanks for riding along and visiting.  We&#8217;re having fun and intend to keep posting to share a little of our time here.  Please leave any comments below and be sure to subscribe to our You Tube channel so you always get the latest videos as soon as they are published.  I usually try to make the videos available here via the blog as well.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Until next time, best wishes to you and yours from HerbnKathy<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Fort Peck &#8211; It&#8217;s a small town (about 250 residents) and it&#8217;s an Army Corp of Engineers &#8220;Project&#8221; that includes a dam, a campground, an Interpretive Center, and multiple fishing\/boating\/hunting recreation areas. Oh, and I almost forgot &#8230; the reason all of the above is here &#8230; the Fort Peck Reservoir. The reservoir (man made &hellip; <a href=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/2019\/05\/08\/so-just-what-is-fort-peck\/\" class=\"more-link\">Continue reading <span class=\"screen-reader-text\">So Just What Is Fort Peck?<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[13403,264,19546,554707329,30025],"tags":[554707735,554707232,554707266,554707744,554707740,554707743,554707738,554707742,554707741,554707704,554707661,18086,554707695,554707739,554707745,554707431],"class_list":["post-5821","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-camping","category-learning","category-sightseeing","category-traveling","category-working","tag-army-corps","tag-camping","tag-dam","tag-dinosaur","tag-downstream","tag-engineers","tag-fort-peck","tag-ft-peck","tag-glasgow","tag-herbnkathy","tag-herbnkathyrv","tag-lake","tag-montana","tag-reservoir","tag-t-rex","tag-workamping"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/5821","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/1"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=5821"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/5821\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":5835,"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/5821\/revisions\/5835"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=5821"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/categories?post=5821"},{"taxonomy":"post_tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/tags?post=5821"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}